  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Response to Freetown Flooding</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On the 2nd August 2019, the city of Freetown experienced the heaviest recorded rainfall since the beginning of this century, leading to the loss of lives and properties worth millions of dollars (ONS - Office of National Security). Even though Freetown was bracing itself for possible flooding, the City Council was not fully prepared for the level of damage. Fifteen deaths have been reported, 5,000 homes were flooded, 20 houses were completely destroyed  and an estimated number of 20,000 people affected as a result. The City of Freetown is expected to experience similar amounts of rainfall over the next six weeks, which is worrisome for both its residents and the ONS. ONS, the coordinating body in charge of disaster response, together with the City Council, have expressed serious concerns over the health situation for those affected by the floods, warning that water and sanitation must be urgently addressed in order to mitigate the risk of a cholera epidemic, a common occurrence during annual urban flooding.</narrative>
  </description>
